Start 64-bit PowerPC CPUs in hypervisor mode, as reset intends.

ppc_cpu_reset() builds an MSR with HV set, but hreg_store_msr() only lets HV
change when the current MSR already has it, and the MSR is still 0 at reset.
The CPU therefore came up in non-hypervisor real mode.  On ISA 3.0 CPUs (the
default POWER10, and POWER9) that mode translates every instruction fetch
through the VRMA's hashed page table, which Unicorn never sets up, so the first
fetch raised a Hypervisor Instruction Storage interrupt (exception 70) and no
PPC64 code ever ran.  Unicorn's MSR register write deliberately cannot set HV,
so callers had no way around it.

Seed HV before the store; msr_mask still strips it on CPUs that lack it, and
MSR_HVB is 0 in the PPC32 build.

diff --git a/qemu/target/ppc/translate_init.inc.c b/qemu/target/ppc/translate_init.inc.c
index d2fb197..01b154b 100644
--- a/qemu/target/ppc/translate_init.inc.c
+++ b/qemu/target/ppc/translate_init.inc.c
@@ -10140,6 +10140,14 @@ static void ppc_cpu_reset(CPUState *dev)
     }
 #endif
 
+    /*
+     * hreg_store_msr() only lets HV change when the MSR already has it, and
+     * the MSR is still 0 here, so the HV bit requested above was dropped.
+     * That left 64-bit Book3S CPUs in non-hypervisor real mode, where ISA 3.0
+     * translates every fetch through a hashed page table that Unicorn never
+     * sets up.  Seed HV; msr_mask still strips it on CPUs that lack it.
+     */
+    env->msr |= MSR_HVB;
     hreg_store_msr(env, msr, 1);
 
     env->nip = env->hreset_vector | env->excp_prefix;
